class.description:
Global Transmit works like signals, without needing a connection between the object that sends and the one that receives. A transmission goes out on a [i]channel[/i], a name ([String] or [StringName]) or a number ([int]), and carries any number of arguments. Everything listening on that channel is called with those arguments. Transmissions can be sent from code with [method transmit], or from an animation with a Transmit track (see [constant Animation.TYPE_TRANSMIT]), whose keys each send a transmission when the animation passes them. Anything can listen: a [Callable] passed to [method listen], usually in [method Node._ready], or a node set up in the editor's [b]Transmit[/b] tab, next to the Signals and Groups tabs, which lists the channels the selected node listens to and the method each one calls. Such a node starts listening when it enters the scene tree while the game runs and stops when it leaves it. [codeblock] # A door that opens when any switch, cutscene or animation transmits "open_vault". func _ready(): GlobalTransmit.listen(&"open_vault", _on_open_vault)
func _on_open_vault(speed = 1.0): $AnimationPlayer.play("open", -1, speed)
# Anywhere else: GlobalTransmit.transmit(&"open_vault", 2.0) GlobalTransmit.transmit(7, "power", true) # Channel number 7. [/codeblock] Delivery is immediate, in the order the listeners started listening; use [method transmit_deferred] to deliver at the end of the frame instead. A listener that takes fewer arguments than were sent receives the first ones, so receivers only declare what they use. There is no fixed limit on the number of arguments; each one costs as much as an argument of a method call. Listeners whose object was freed are dropped automatically. [b]Note:[/b] Name and number channels are separate: the channel [code]7[/code] and the channel [code]"7"[/code] are different channels.
